    "factsSummary": "This is an appeal against the decision of the Edo State High Court, Benin, delivered by Omage, J. Respondent's case was that, his father was, until his death in 1950 the owner of a large farm situate at Ekoken in Orio Village in Benin Judicial Division. The respondent's father inherited the land from his father called Osagie who was the original founder and settler of the land in dispute. On the death of Osagie, the respondent's grandfather, the respondent's father as the eldest son inherited the land and planted rubber trees. When the respondent's father died in 1950, the respondent performed the burial customary ceremony rites and ceremonies and, in accordance with the Bini custom inherited the farm and the rubber plantation which he continued to farm. The respondent continued to farm on the land without any disturbance until sometime in 1959, when the appellant's father, one David Amayo, encroached on the rubber plantation. Respondent instituted an action against the appellant's father in the Grade 'A' Customary Court where the respondent obtained judgment. In 1979, the appellant, purportedly claiming through his father, broke into the farmland claiming part of it and commenced farming. The plaintiff/respondent sued the defendant/appellant and claimed as follows:\"1. Declaration that the plaintiff has possessory title to that piece of land situate at Orio village, Benin, within Benin Judicial Division and therefore the plaintiff is entitled to obtain a certificate of occupancy in respect of the land, shown on plan No. BEC 34.2. N1000. (One thousand Naira) damages for trespass on the said land in that in January, 1979, the defendant broke into the said land and started (sic) farming thereon without plaintiffs consent.3. An order of perpetual injunction restraining the defendant, his servants or agents from entering the said land without the plaintiff's authority.\"The appellant's case on the other hand is that the appellant as the eldest surviving son of his father buried his father in accordance with the Bini native law and custom. It was the case of the appellant that the respondent, having sued and lost in a suit is estopped from bringing any action for declaration and trespass and injunction against the appellant as the matter is res judicata. Pleadings were filed and duly exchanged. After trial, the learned trial Judge found for the respondent, granting him all his claims with costs. Dissatisfied, appellant appealed to the Court of Appeal.",
    "decisionSummary": "In the final analysis, the Court of Appeal dismissed the appeal and affirmed the decision of the trial Court.",
    "ratioDecidendi": [
      "CUSTOMARY LAW - BENIN/BINI LAND TENURE SYSTEM - Procedure for acquisition of title to land under Bini customary law\n\"In Bello v. Eweka (1981) 1 SC 101 at pages 127-128, the Supreme Court had this to say: \"Now, in Atiti Gold v. Beatrice Osaseren (supra), this Court approved the requirements of customary law for obtaining valid titles to native lands in Benin as set out by Idigbe, CJ (Mid-West State as he then was), from whose Court the appeal came to the Supreme Court in that case. The requirements as set out by the learned Chief Justice are as follows:- \"A native may occupy land (i.e. any land not already in previous occupation of any individual) for farming purposes, and although such an 'occupier' is usually loosely referred to as an 'owner' he is not under Bini custom the legal owner until he has got the approval of the Oba of Benin to 'own' that land: this is because basically all land in Benin is owned by the community for whom the Oba of Benin holds the same in trust and, it is the Oba of Benin who can transfer to any individual the ownership of such land and until so transferred the occupier may continue to hold it for purposes of farming only. When therefore anyone wants or wished to own such land for purposes other than farming, i.e. of true ownership, that person must apply to the Oba through the plot allotment committee for ownership of it. It appears from the evidence that the procedure for obtaining complete ownership of such land by a non-occupier is this: the applicant must first negotiate with the occupier (usually farmer) to buy the occupier's interest (i.e. his crops) in the land and having settled with the occupier, he must then apply to the plot allotment committee who should satisfy that negotiation for transfer or purchase of the crops thereon has been settled thereafter.\" In Bello's case, the Court resolved a dispute of competing rights of parties who purchased the land from the same vendor and in doing so considered the issue of Oba's approval. The issue of title to land is different from the present case which is a case of inheritance based on the long possession or ownership by the respondent's family. In addition, the respondents claim was for possessory rights and the Court granted the respondent possessory right as claimed. With respect to the learned Senior Advocate of Nigeria, for the appellant, I am of the view that the case of Okeaya v. Aguebor (1970) 1 All NLR 8-9, setting out the procedure for acquiring title to land in Benin Kingdom is not applicable to the instant case in that title was acquired by the respondent's ancestors before 1961 before the setting up of the Allocation Committee, referred to in Okeaya (supra). In the instant case, the respondent cannot be regarded as an applicant for title to land who is required to submit an application to the Oba through the Allocation Committee as the land in dispute and the rubber plantation is a family property which has been in long possession of the respondent's family. As shown by the evidence before the trial Court, the respondent as the eldest son of his late father, having buried his father, inherited the farm and the rubber plantation. In other words, the farm and rubber plantation was originally owned by the grand-father then father of the respondent devolved on the respondent as the eldest male son of his father. I agree completely with the learned Senior Advocate of Nigeria that before a person can acquire title to land in Benin Kingdom, the approval of the Oba, must first be obtained. In my humble opinion the approval is only required in respect of new title to land through purchase or allocation but does not include land inherited such as in this case. We have not been referred to any authority which provides that the Oba's approval must be obtained before a person can inherit the property of his deceased parents. It should be noted that the title of the respondent's grandfather and father was never challenged by anybody.\"\nPer BABA ALKALI BA'ABA ,JCA (Pp. 7-8, paras. B-B)"
    ],
